   "confidence": 0.8,
   "curator_note": "Book draws 2 arcs for this branch on the molecular ion (the solid fishhook from the odd electron on O -- the same arc that serves the Eq. 9.19 branch -- and a dashed fishhook from the C1-C6 ring bond into the C1-O bond); moves <1,2> are drawn, move <3> is an implicit homolysis hook required for electron balance (not drawn on the page). Ring alpha-cleavage is intramolecular, so the product is a single ring-opened distonic ion of unchanged m/z 114, not two fragments."
  },
  {
   "evidence_id": "evidence_step_2",
   "source_id": "mclafferty_ims_4e",
   "target_type": "step",
   "target_id": "step_alpha_loss_ethene_1",
   "support_type": "textbook_proposed",
   "locator": {
    "page": 243,
    "equation": "9.20",
    "figure": null,
    "panel": null,
    "section": "9.2 Alcohols"
   },
   "confidence": 0.8,
   "curator_note": "Book draws 2 arcs on the folded ring-opened ion, in DASHED lines (the dashed pair is the second branch of the same structure) (a fishhook from the odd electron into the forming pi bond and a fishhook from the breaking C-C sigma bond into that same pi bond); moves <1,2> are drawn, move <3> is an implicit homolysis hook required for electron balance (not drawn on the page). The odd electron at C6 forms the C6=C5 pi bond while the C5-C4 bond is homolysed, expelling ethene and leaving the distonic m/z 86 ion, as labelled '- C2H4' on the page."
  },
  {
   "evidence_id": "evidence_step_3",
   "source_id": "mclafferty_ims_4e",
   "target_type": "step",
   "target_id": "step_alpha_loss_ethene_2",
   "support_type": "textbook_proposed",
   "locator": {
    "page": 243,
    "equation": "9.20",
    "figure": null,
    "panel": null,
    "section": "9.2 Alcohols"
   },
   "confidence": 0.8,
   "curator_note": "Book draws 2 arcs on the m/z 86 ion (a fishhook from the odd electron into the forming pi bond and a fishhook from the breaking C-C sigma bond into that same pi bond); moves <1,2> are drawn, move <3> is an implicit homolysis hook required for electron balance (not drawn on the page). The odd electron at C4 forms the C4=C3 pi bond while the C3-C2 bond is homolysed, expelling a second ethene and leaving the distonic m/z 58 ion (CH3-.CH-CH=OH+). The first ethene is carried along as a spectator so the step stays atom-balanced."
  },
  {
   "evidence_id": "evidence_mod_rules",
   "source_id": "mod_rules",
   "target_type": "mechanism",
   "target_id": "IMS9-EQ9.20b",
   "support_type": "curator_inferred",
   "locator": {
    "page": null,
    "equation": null,
    "figure": null,
    "panel": null,
    "section": "src/data_generation/rules/"
   },
   "confidence": 0.5,
   "curator_note": "No MOD rule is cross-linked: data/mechanisms/rule_index.json has no by_equation entry for 9.19 or 9.20 (the MOD corpus covers Chapters 4 and 8 only), and no rule elsewhere in src/data_generation/rules/ encodes alpha-cleavage of a saturated ALCOHOL radical cation. The two nearest candidates were checked against this substrate and do NOT match: IMS_4_13_1/IMS_4_13_2 ('radical induced (alpha-)cleavage for a saturated site') require [O+.] flanked by two carbons (an ether radical cation) and split the substrate into two components, whereas here O+. carries an H and the cleavage is intramolecular ring opening; IMS_4_26_1 has the same ether requirement through its [_A] placeholders (all [_A] in one rule must unify to the same element). IMS_4_17/IMS_4_17_1-3 do cleave a C-C bond at a carbinol carbon bearing [O+.], but leave the charge/odd electron on oxygen and never form the C=O+ pi bond, so they do not describe the electron flow drawn in Eq. 9.19/9.20."
  },
